<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_10-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Comparing multiple means: Theory-based approach</title>

<subsection> 
 <title>Comparing popular diets</title>
<p>Because about two-thirds of Americans are considered overweight, weight loss is big business. 
There are many different types of diets, but do some work better than others? Is low fat better 
than low carb or is some combination best? Researchers 
(<url href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.297.9.969">Garnder et al. 2007</url>) conducted a study 
involving four popular diets: Atkins (very low carb), Zone (40:30:30 ratio of carbs, protein, 
fat), LEARN (high carbohydrate, low fat), and Ornish (low fat). They randomly assigned women aged 
25-50 with a body mass index (BMI) of 27-40 (overweight and obese) to one of the four diets. The 
311 women who volunteered for the program were educated on their assigned diet and were observed 
periodically as they stayed on the diet for a year. At the end of the year, the researchers calculated 
the change in BMI (e.g., negative means reduction in BMI) for each woman and compared the results 
across the four diets.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Step 1: Ask a research question</title>
<activity>

 <!--Question 1-->
<statement>
<p>What is the overarching research question the researchers hoped to answer?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Is there evidence of an association between change in BMI and type of diet used?
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Step 2: Design a study and collect data</title>

<activity>
  <!--Question 2a-->
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What are the observational units in this study?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Each of the 311 women in the study
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <!--Question 2b-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        Identify the explanatory and response variables. Classify them as categorical or quantitative. For categorical variables, indicate how many categories are used.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Explanatory variable: diet (Atkins, Zone, Ornish, LEARN); categorical with 4 categories, Response: change in BMI; quantitative
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
  <task>
    <!--Question 3a-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        Does this study make use of random sampling, random assignment, both, or neither? What are the implications of your answer with regard to scope of inference?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Random assignment (yes), random sampling (no), Cause and effect is possible but not generalization to a population.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <!--Question 3b-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        Did the researchers collect the data as paired data or as independent samples? In other words, according to the study design, are the responses from one treatment group paired with or independent of the responses from other treatment groups?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Responses from one treatment group are independent of the responses from another treatment group.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 4-->
  <statement>
    <p>State the null and alternative hypotheses, both in words and symbols, for testing 
    the research conjecture. (Recall that the response variable is change in BMI; positive 
    values indicate an increase in BMI and negative values indicate a decrease in BMI 
    from the beginning to the end of the study.)</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Null hypothesis: The long-run mean change in BMI is the same for all three diets (<m>\mu_{\text{Atkins}}=\mu_{\text{Zone}}=\mu_{\text{Ornish}}=\mu_{\text{LEARN}}</m>)
    </p>
    <p>
      Alternative hypothesis: At least one of the means (<m>\mu_{\text{Atkins}}, \mu_{\text{Zone}}=\mu_{\text{Ornish}}, \mu_{\text{LEARN}}</m>) is different than the others.
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Step 3: Explore the data</title>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The data for this investigation appear in a file called <url href="https://static-content2.zybooks.com/isi/data/chap9/Diets.txt">Diets</url>. Copy and paste the data into the <url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/anovashuffle/AnovaShuffle.htm?hideExtras=2"> Multiple Means applet</url>. (Recall that the response variable is change in BMI.) Press <em>Use Data</em>, and the applet will produce dotplots and summary statistics.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Report the mean and standard deviation of the BMI values in this sample.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        total mean = -0.966, standard deviation = 2.209, n = 311
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Check the Show Groups box. You can also check the Boxplots box to overlay those as well. Report the means, standard deviations, and sample sizes for the change in BMI amounts for each diet.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Atkins: mean = -1.65, standard deviation = 2.54, n = 77;
      </p>
      <p>
        Zone: mean = -0.53, standard deviation = 2.00, n = 79;
      </p>
      <p>
        Ornish: mean = -0.77, standard deviation = 2.14, n = 76,
      </p>
      <p>
        LEARN: mean = -0.92, standard deviation = 2.00, n=79.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Describe what the graphs and statistics reveal about whether the diets appear to differ with regard to change in BMI amounts and, if so, which diet appears to be best and which worst.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The greatest change in BMI occurs in the Atkins group (-1.65 average change in BMI), while the other three diets appear to have similar change (-0.92, -0.77, -0.53 for LEARN, Ornish and Zone, respectively).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Step 4: Draw inferences beyond the data</title>

<activity>
 <!--Question 6-->
  <statement>
    <p>
      Conduct a simulation-based randomization test, as was done in the last section, to compare the four group means using the mean of the absolute values of the group differences (Mean Group Diff) as the statistic. Report the observed value of this statistic. Then determine and report a p-value based on at least 1,000 shuffles.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Mean Group Diff = 0.585, p-value is around 0.008
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 7-->
  <statement>
    <p>Based on this p-value, evaluate the strength of evidence against the null hypothesis provided 
    by the experimental data. In other words, do the data provide strong evidence that at least one 
    of the four diets differs with regard to average change in BMI?</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      We have very strong evidence against the null and in support of an association between diet and average change in BMI.
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<p>Although the Mean Group Diff statistic is fairly easy to understand and calculate, it is not 
commonly used, in part because there is no theory-based model for the null distribution. Another 
downside is that the Mean Group Diff statistic is not standardized so it is not comparable 
across studies (e.g., a Mean Group Diff statistic of 1 in one study might be strong evidence but 
in another study might not show convincing evidence against the null hypothesis).</p>

<remark> 
<p>Instead of comparing only the differences in means, a statistic could standardize those differences 
by comparing to the amount of within-group or natural variability in the response variable while also 
taking sample sizes into account.</p>
</remark>

<p>
  A much more commonly used statistic for comparing multiple groups on a quantitative response, which does have a theory-based distribution, is called an <term>F-statistic</term>. As with the Mean Group Diff statistic, the F-statistic equals zero only when the group means are all identical. Otherwise the F-statistic is positive, with larger values indicating larger differences across the group means.
</p>

<p>
  The F-statistic (see Formula section at the end of Example 9.2 for details) is a ratio of <q>between-group</q> and <q>within-group</q> variability. Thus,
  <me>
    F = \frac{\textbf{Between-group variability}}{\textbf{Within-group variability}}
  </me>
</p>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Select the <em>Statistic</em> pull-down menu above the null distribution to select <em>F-statistic</em>, and the null distribution changes from the Mean Group Diff statistic to the F-statistic.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <!--Question 8a-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        Report the observed value of the F-statistic, which appears on the left side of the applet.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>F = 3.80</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task xml:id="q-10-2-8-b">
    <!--Question 8b-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the simulation results and the observed value of the F-statistic to estimate the p-value.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        p-value <m>\approx 0.012</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <!--Question 8c-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        Is this p-value similar to the one based on the Mean Group Diff statistic? Is your conclusion about strength of evidence the same with both statistics?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes, the p-value is similar and so the conclusion is also similar.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<p>The F-statistic has a known theoretical distribution when the null hypothesis is true, 
called (surprisingly enough) an <term>F-distribution</term>.</p>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Check the box to Overlay F distribution. 
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <!--Question 9a-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        Would you say there is good agreement between this theoretical prediction and your simulated null distribution?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <!--Question 9b-->
    <statement>
      <p>
        How does the theory-based p-value compare to what you found in <xref ref="q-10-2-8-b"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Theory-based p-value=0.0106, the p-values are similar.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<p>But what does F = 3.80 really mean? Check the box by <em>Show ANOVA Table</em> to 
see more output related to the calculation of the value of the F-statistic.</p>

<activity xml:id="q-10-2-10">
 <!--Question 10-->
  <statement>
    <p>Confirm that the F-statistic is the ratio of the mean square for treatment 
    to the mean square for error.  These are the numbers in the <c>MS</c> column of the ANOVA table.  The mean square for treatment is in the <c>Model</c> row, and the mean square for error is in the <c>Error</c> row.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      <m>\frac{18.06}{4.75} = 3.80</m>
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<p>
  The next series of exercises will take a closer look at how the F-statistic is calculated.  It will be easiest to do this with a spreadsheet, but a calculator will still work.
</p>

