  <statement>
    <p>
      Now we'll fill in the SS (SSE) column, the Sum Squared Error.  For the first number, the <c>Model</c> row, compute the sum of four Squared Error (SE) numbers from the last exercise.  For the last number, the <c>Total</c> row, compute <m>s^2 \times n</m>, in other words, the standard deviation of the entire sample times the sample size of the whole sample.  For the middle entry, corresponding to the <c>Error</c> row, compute <m>\text{SS}_{\text{Total}}-\text{SS}_{\text{Model}}</m>, in other words, the last entry of this column minus the first entry.
    </p>
    <p>
      Just like part a, your three numbers should be such that the number from the <c>Model</c> row plus the number from the <c>Error</c> row add up to the number from the <c>Total</c> row.
    </p>
  </statement>
