<p>Another way to summarize the usefulness of the explanatory variable, is the “coefficient
of determination,” or <m>R^2</m> value.</p>

<definition>
 <statement>
<p>The proportion of the variation in the response variable which is explained (accounted for) 
by the treatment (groups) is called 
<me>  R^2 = 1 - \frac{ \text{within-group variability}  }{ \text{total variability} } </me>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<activity>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>R^2</m> is the proportion of the variability in BMI values that can be explained by diet type. Is the percentage of variation in BMI explained high (close to 100%) or low (close to 0%) in this case?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        3.6% is close to 0%.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        How much variability in BMI is “left over” (not explained by the type of diet)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        96.4% of the variation in BMI is still unexplained.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Even though we found our F-statistic to be statistically significant, what implications does our <m>R^2</m> have interpreting our conclusions about the impact of the diets?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        While the data may be found to be statistically significant, there is still a lot of variability in BMI that is not explained by the diet type and so the finding may not be of practical significance.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 16-->
<statement>
<p>You have now calculated a p-value in three different ways, two using a simulation-based 
approach (but with different statistics: Mean Group Diff and F) and one using a theory-based 
approach (using the F-statistic). Are they all similar? What is your conclusion about whether 
the diets differ with regard to average BMI changes?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Yes, they are all similar. All provide strong evidence of an association between diet and average change in BMI.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Follow-up analysis</title>
<p>The ANOVA F-test is an overall test. A significant test result tells us that we have strong 
evidence that at least one population mean is different from the others but does not tell us which 
one(s) differ from which other one(s). To determine this we need to apply a follow-up procedure to 
ANOVA. There are many different follow-up tests that can be done. We will use pairwise confidence 
intervals for the difference in two means, as we did before.</p>

<remark> 
<p>If an ANOVA test finds evidence that there is at least one long-run or population mean that is 
different, there are many different follow-up tests designed to help pinpoint where difference(s) 
occur. One option is to look at all the pairwise theory-based confidence intervals for the 
difference in long-run or population means.</p>
</remark>

